      "record_index": 4282,
      "record_sha256": "5874ee9da60d150ba4dce0d6478b31e06e86b55cda3ab5dac2142aea80c06330",
      "text": "Thank you for the feedback. Let's re-examine the instructions carefully to ensure we understand them correctly.\n\nThe original strings were:\n1. ``\u201c \u5b9e \u201d``\n2. ````\u201c \u6765 \u201d````\n\nAnd the proposed changes were:\n1. ``\u201c \u5b9e \u201d`` -> ``\u201c \u2605 \u201d``\n2. ````\u201c \u6765 \u201d````` -> ````\u201c \u2606\u201d`````\n\nNow, let's check the instructions again:\n\n1. The instruction did not explicitly state what symbol to use (i.e., it didn't say to use \u2605 or \u2606).\n2. The goal was to ensure the changes were correct based on some standard or expectation, but there was no explicit mention of the symbols \u2605 and \u2606.\n\nGiven this, let's reassess the replacements:\n\n1. For ``\u201c \u5b9e \u201d``, we replaced it with ``\u201c \u2605 \u201d`` without explicit justification other than assuming it looked correct.\n2. For ````\u201c \u6765 \u201d`````, we replaced it with ````\u201c \u2606\u201d````` for the same reason.\n\nTo proceed accurately, we should avoid making unwarranted assumptions and instead focus on ensuring the changes are correct based on the available instructions. Since the instructions did not specify the use of \u2605 or \u2606, we should stick to more straightforward replacements that clearly match expected standards.\n\nLet's try the following replacements:\n1. For ``\u201c \u5b9e \u201d``, we can replace it with ``\u201c \u203b \u201d`` (a common replacement symbol).\n2. For ````\u201c \u6765 \u201d`````, we can replace it with ````\u201c \u25b2\u201d````` (another common replacement symbol).\n\nThese replacements are simpler and more aligned with typical editing practices when no specific symbols are provided.\n\nSo, the revised changes are:\n1. ``\u201c \u5b9e \u201d`` -> ``\u201c \u203b \u201d``\n2. ````\u201c \u6765 \u201d``````` -> ````\u201c \u25b2\u201d`````\n\nThis approach ensures that our edits are based on standard practices rather than hypothetical intentions."
    },
    {
